<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01562</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6d37e43e-8618-4927-84f4-0dce7ee8c55b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity provides students practice in adding and subtracting different polynomial expressions. Students will first add and subtract monomials.</para>
<para>Students will then move to adding and subtracting larger polynomial expressions by focusing on combining like terms within the polynomials. For an additional challenge, have students simplify the polynomial expression under the Extending Your Thinking heading.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Answer the first two questions in the activity as a class. As students analyze the terms in question 1, \(6x^4y^2\) and \(8x^4y^2\), stress that only like terms, with the same variables having the same degree, can be added and subtracted.</para>
<para>Allow a moment for students to think about question 2. After soliciting the class for an answer, emphasize that when like terms are added or subtracted, the variables and exponents remain the same while the coefficients are added or subtracted.</para>
<para>Allow students to complete the remaining questions individually.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->Representation: Illustrate Connections</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content--><para>Use different color highlighters to help show the terms that have the same variables with the same exponents.   You can also use colored index cards to break down expressions by putting all of the terms with same variable and same exponents on different colors.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Supports accessibility for Organizing, Visual-Spatial Processing</para>
    <!-- Extra Support Content--> 
  </div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Solve these two problems as a class.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Do these terms have the same variables with the same exponents? Explain your answer. </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes. Both terms have the same variables raised to the same degrees: \(x\) is raised to the fourth power, and \(y\) is squared.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add the terms together: \(6x^4y^2+8x^4y^2\). What is the sum? </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(14x^4y^2\)</para>
<para>In problems 3 - 7, simplify the monomial addition and subtraction problems on your own.  Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(12q+9q\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(21q\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(-14c^3+5c^3\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(-9c^3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(8g^2h^2+(-6g^2h^2)\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2g^2h^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(8mn^3p-(-5mn^3p)\) </item>
  <para>Students also see this instrucitonal video here:</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
    <iframe>
      <video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
        <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba5bc330fb9f7f706e35cf5a38cdfedb85f34aec">
        <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/712187a19365bfc0825bd798bf9f258c6ec2c971" srclang="en_us">
      </track></source></video>
    </iframe>
  </div>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(13mn^3p\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(-16a^2+3b^2+8a^2 \)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(-8a^2+3b^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> Can the following monomials be simplified using like terms? Explain. </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No, the monomial terms do not contain the same variables with the same exponents. The first term has the variable \(b\) with an exponent of 1. The second term has the variable \(b\) with an exponent of 2. The monomials are not like terms.</para>
<para>In problems 9 - 13, simplify the polynomial addition and subtraction problems on your own.  Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( (5k+7m-2)+(7k+9m-4)\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(12k+16m-6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((12w-11)-(5w+6) \)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(7w-5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((9h^2-4h+17)+(2h^2-9) \)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(11h^2-4h+8\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((8f^2+6fv-3v^2)-(6f^2-3v^2)\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2f^2+6fv\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((8z^3-6zx+3x^2)+(12z^3-4zx)\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(20z^3-10zx+3x^2\)</para>
<title> Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Simplify. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<para>\((6a^3-2a^2b)-2(ab^2+3b^3)+(2a^2b+2ab^2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(6a^3-6b^3\)</para>
<para>Here is how to solve this problem:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Distribute the multiplication over addition.</para>
<para>  \(6a^3-2a^2b-2ab^2-6b^3+2a^2b+2ab^2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together.</para>
<para>  \(6a^3-2a^2b+2a^2b-2ab^2+2ab^2-6b^3\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para>  \(6a^3-6b^3\)</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students may think that they can combine any terms that share the same variables. Emphasize that each variable in the terms must also share the same degree for the terms to be added or subtracted.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>As students add and subtract to simplify the polynomial expressions, emphasize that the degree of the term does not change. When \(5x^2\) is added to \(6x^2\), the sum still has a degree of two, \(11x^2\). The same is true for the difference between two like terms.</para>
<para>Invite students to share any of the problems that gave them difficulty. Work through the addition or subtraction as a class.</para>
<title> 6.1.2: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Find the sum.</para>
<para>\((14y^2+6y-4)+(3y^2+8y+5)\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">ANSWERS</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">FEEDBACK</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(17y^2+14y+1\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: By combining like terms, you can add the polynomials.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(11y^2-2y-9\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: You need to add the polynomials instead of subtracting them. The answer is \(17y^2+14y+1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(17y^2+14y-9\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the constants that you added. The answer is \(17y^2+14y+1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(11y^2+14y+1\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check your addition on the terms to the second degree. The answer is \(17y^2+14y+1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title> 6.1.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Adding and Subtracting Monomials</title>
<para>We have learned how to simplify expressions by combining like terms. Remember, like terms must have the same variables with the same exponent. Since monomials are terms, adding and subtracting monomials is the same as combining like terms. If the monomials are like terms, we just combine them by adding or subtracting the coefficients. Remember that like terms must have the same variables with the same exponents.</para>
<para>These terms have the same variables and the same exponents:</para>
<list>
  <item>\(5m^3n^2\)          \(2m^3n^2\)</item>
</list>
<para>So we can add the monomials:</para>
<list>
  <item>\(5m^3n^2 + 2m^3n^2 = 7m^3n^2\)</item>
</list>
<para>The variables and exponents of the monomials do not change when we add or subtract them.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>

<para>\(18a+2a\) </para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>

<para>\(18a+2a = 20a\) </para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>

<para>\(37t-15t\) </para>
<emphasis>
</emphasis>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>

<para>\(37t-15t =22t\) </para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>\(25y2+15y^2\) </para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para>\(25y^2+15y^2=40y^2\) </para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 4</para>
<para>16y2+6y+15y2+3y </para>
<emphasis>
</emphasis>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para>\(16y^2+6y+15y^2+3y =30y^2+9y\)</para>

<title>Try It: Adding and Subtracting Monomials</title>
<para>Add or subtract.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( 25y^2+15y^2\) </item>
  <item> \(16pq^3-(-7pq^3)\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>Solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to add and subtract:</para>
<para>Combine like terms to simplify the expressions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( 25y^2+15y^2=40y^2\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\begin{array}{rcl}16pq^3-(-7pq^3)&amp;=&amp;16pq^3+7pq^3\\&amp;=&amp;23pq^3\end{array}\)</item>
</list>
<title>Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
<para>We can think of adding and subtracting polynomials as just adding and subtracting a series of monomials. Look for the like terms—those with the same variables and the same exponent. The Commutative Property allows us to rearrange the terms to put like terms together.</para>
<para>Let’s look at some examples.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>Find the sum: \((7y^2-2y+9)+(4y^2-8y-7)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms by finding those with the same variable and same exponent.</para>
<para>  \(({\style{color:red}7}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y} + 9) + ({\style{color:red}4}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}y} - 7)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together. Be sure to take the appropriate sign with each term \((+/-)\).</para>
<para>  \({\style{color:red}7}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} + {\style{color:red}4}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y} - {\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}y} + 9 - 7\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para>  \({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}0}{\style{color:blue}y} + 2\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>

<para>Find the sum: \((22g-14h+3)+(10g+8h-9)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Identify like terms.</para>

<para>\(({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+3)+({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}h}-9)\)</para>

<para>Color 22g, 10g in red.  14h, 8h in blue.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together. Be sure to take the appropriate sign with each term (+/−).</para>

<para>\(({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+{\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}h} +3+9)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>

<para>\(32g-6h +12\)
</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>

<para>Find the sum: \((8j+7k)+(2s^2-3k+26)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Identify like terms.</para>
<para>\((8j+{\style{color:blue}7}{\style{color:blue}k}-3)+({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}s}^{\style{color:red}2}-{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}k}+26)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>

<para>\({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}s}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}7}{\style{color:blue}k}-{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}k}+8j-3+26\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 4</para>

<para>Find the difference: \((15g-4h+8)-(10g+13h-1)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>

<para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+8)-({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}h}-1)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>
<para>Remember to distribute the subtraction to all terms in the second polymnomial.</para>

<para>\({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}h}+8+1\)</para>


<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para>\(5g-17h+9\)</para>


<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 5</para>

<para>Find the difference: \((16y^2+12y+9)-(3y^2+2y-12) \)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>

<para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}6}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}+9)-({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}-12) \)</para>


<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>

<para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}6}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}-{\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}-{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}+9+12) \)</para>


<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>

<para>\((13y^2+10y+21) \)</para>
<title>Try It: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
<para>Find the sum and difference. If necessary, be careful with the signs as you distribute while subtracting the polynomials.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((7x^2-4x+5)+(x^2-7x+3)\) </item>
  <item>\((9w^2-7w+5)-(2w^2-4)\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the<emphasis> Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title> Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to find the sum and difference:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((7x^2-4x+5)+(x^2-7x+3)=8x^2-11x+8\)</item>
  <item>\(\begin{array}{rcl}(9w^2-7w+5)-(2w^2-4)&amp;=&amp;(9w^2-7w+5)-2w^2+4\\&amp;=&amp;7w^2-7w+9\end{array}\)</item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
</document>
